thor\nL184:   * Venue\nL185:   * Institution\nL186:   * Topic\nL187: \nL188: About arXivLabs\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2510.12032v1] Multi-stage Prompt Refinement for Mitigating Hallucinations in Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.12032v1)\nciteturn2840view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.12032v1\",\"lineno\":15}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 14 Oct 2025]\nL9: # Title:Multi-stage Prompt Refinement for Mitigating Hallucinations in Large Language Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Jung-Woo Shim , cite6†Yeong-Joon Ju , cite7†Ji-Hoon Park , cite8†Seong-Whan Lee L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Multi-stage Prompt Refinement for Mitigating Hallucinations in Large Language Models, by Jung-Woo Shim and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) have shown strong performance in natural language understanding and generation tasks. However, LLMs continue to encounter challenges with hallucinations, where models generate plausible but incorrect information. While several factors contribute to hallucinations, the impact of ill-formed prompts, prompts with ambiguous wording, incorrect grammar, or incomplete information, was relatively under explored.\nL17: To address this, we introduce Multi-stage Prompt Refinement (MPR), a framework designed to systematically improve these ill-formed prompts across multiple stages. Each stage addresses specific errors such as punctuation, typographical mistakes, and misuse of key terms, using small language models (SLMs) fine-tuned for these tasks. MPR iteratively enhances the clarity of prompts with additional context and employs a self-reflection mechanism with ranking to prioritize the most relevant input.\nL18: Experimental results on hallucination benchmarks show that prompts refined by MPR achieve over an 85~\\% win rate compared to their original forms, demonstrating its effectiveness in reducing hallucinations and improving LLM output accuracy. Interestingly, we reveal that MPR can be combined with existing post-hoc hallucination mitigation frameworks, further enhancing its versatility. MPR provides a lightweight and adaptable solution for enhancing LLM reliability across various domains.\nL19: Comments:  | 22 pages, 6 figures\nL20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite11†arXiv:2510.12032 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite12†arXiv:2510.12032v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.12032†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Jungwoo Shim [cite14†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Tue, 14 Oct 2025 00:31:36 UTC (283 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Multi-stage Prompt Refinement for Mitigating Hallucinations in Large Language Models, by Jung-Woo Shim and 3 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite9†View PDF L36:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite15†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite16†view license†creativecommons.org L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.CL\nL43: \nL44: cite17†< prev |   cite18†next > L45: \nL46: cite19†new | cite20†recent | cite21†2025-10 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite22†cs L51: cite23†cs.AI L52: cite24†cs.LG L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.h